% ------------------------------------------------------------------
% Title
% ------------------------------------------------------------------

\title{\textbf{A Cubic-Root-of-Unity Structure for the Kappa Invariant}\
\large Exact $N$-Body Formulae, Elementary-Symmetric Reduction,\
and Controlled-Variable Transitions}

\author{Independent Mathematical Research}
\date{\today}

\begin{document}

\maketitle

\begin{abstract}

We study a family of symmetric polynomial invariants arising from the
ratio
[
P_N =
\frac{\displaystyle\prod_{i=1}^{N}(1+x_i^3)}
{\displaystyle\prod_{i=1}^{N}(1+x_i)},
\qquad
\kappa_N = 1-P_N.
]
Direct symbolic calculations for $N=3,4,5$ exhibit large polynomial
expressions which nevertheless reduce exactly to compact quadratic
forms in the elementary symmetric polynomials
$e_1,\ldots,e_N$.

The central observation is the factorization
[
1+x^3=(1+x)(1-x+x^2),
]
which yields the exact all-$N$ identity
[
P_N=\prod_{i=1}^{N}(1-x_i+x_i^2).
]
Writing $\omega=e^{2\pi i/3}$, this expression admits the
root-of-unity representation
[
P_N=E(\omega)E(\omega^2)
=\left|E(\omega)\right|^2,
\qquad
E(t)=\prod_{i=1}^{N}(1+x_i t)
=\sum_{k=0}^{N}e_k t^k.
]
Consequently, if the elementary symmetric polynomials are grouped
according to their index modulo $3$, the invariant has the universal
form
[
\kappa_N=1-A^2-B^2-C^2+AB+AC+BC.
]

We prove the exact transition law obtained by adjoining a controlled
variable $n$:
[
\kappa_{N+1}-\kappa_N
=====================

n(1-n)(1-\kappa_N).
]
In particular, adjoining $0$ or $1$ leaves the invariant unchanged.
For a two-factor integer $N=pq$, the same structure gives an explicit
quadratic relation involving $p+q$. This suggests a possible route
toward factor recovery if an independently accessible mechanism for
measuring or computing the corresponding invariant can be found.
The paper separates the proven algebraic results from this still-open
algorithmic question.

\end{abstract}

\tableofcontents

\newpage

% ==================================================================
\section{Introduction}
% ==================================================================

The purpose of this paper is to describe an exact algebraic structure
behind a family of symmetric polynomial expressions that initially
appear to grow rapidly in complexity with the number of variables.

For three variables $p,q,r$, define
[
M_{1,3}
=======

(1+p)(1+q)(1+r),
]
and
[
X_3
===

(1+p^3)(1+q^3)(1+r^3).
]
The corresponding normalized quantity is
[
P_3=\frac{X_3}{M_{1,3}},
\qquad
\kappa_3=1-P_3.
]

Analogous definitions are made for arbitrary $N$:
[
M_{1,N}=\prod_{i=1}^{N}(1+x_i),
]
[
X_N=\prod_{i=1}^{N}(1+x_i^3),
]
[
P_N=\frac{X_N}{M_{1,N}},
\qquad
\kappa_N=1-P_N.
]

Direct computer algebra calculations for $N=3,4,5$ give exact
polynomial quotients and verify that the resulting $\kappa_N$ are
symmetric polynomials. Their elementary-symmetric representations are
surprisingly compact.

The main purpose of this paper is to explain why.

The essential identity is elementary:
[
1+x^3=(1+x)(1-x+x^2).
]
It immediately implies
[
P_N=\prod_{i=1}^{N}(1-x_i+x_i^2).
]
The quadratic factor has a second important representation:
[
1-x+x^2=(1+\omega x)(1+\omega^2x),
]
where
[
\omega^3=1,
\qquad
\omega\neq1,
\qquad
1+\omega+\omega^2=0.
]

This leads to a root-of-unity evaluation of the elementary-symmetric
generating polynomial and explains the complete quadratic structure
observed experimentally.

% ==================================================================
\section{Definitions}
% ==================================================================

\begin{definition}[Elementary symmetric polynomials]
For variables $x_1,\ldots,x_N$, define
[
e_0=1
]
and, for $1\leq k\leq N$,
[
e_k
===

\sum_{1\leq i_1<\cdots<i_k\leq N}
x_{i_1}\cdots x_{i_k}.
]
Thus
[
\prod_{i=1}^{N}(1+x_i t)
========================

\sum_{k=0}^{N}e_k t^k.
]
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Kappa invariant]
For $N\geq1$, define
[
M_{1,N}
=======

\prod_{i=1}^{N}(1+x_i),
]
[
X_N
===

\prod_{i=1}^{N}(1+x_i^3),
]
and
[
P_N=\frac{X_N}{M_{1,N}},
\qquad
\kappa_N=1-P_N.
]
\end{definition}

The original expressions written as sums over all subsets are
[
M_{1,N}
=======

\sum_{S\subseteq{1,\ldots,N}}
\prod_{i\in S}x_i
=================

\sum_{k=0}^{N}e_k,
]
and
[
X_N
===

\sum_{k=0}^{N}
e_k(x_1^3,\ldots,x_N^3).
]

% ==================================================================
\section{The Fundamental Factorization}
% ==================================================================

\begin{theorem}[Exact product representation]
For arbitrary $N$,
[
\boxed{
P_N
===

\prod_{i=1}^{N}(1-x_i+x_i^2)
}
]
and therefore
[
\boxed{
\kappa_N
========

1-\prod_{i=1}^{N}(1-x_i+x_i^2).
}
]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
For every variable $x$,
[
1+x^3=(1+x)(1-x+x^2).
]
Therefore
[
X_N
===

# \prod_{i=1}^{N}(1+x_i^3)

\prod_{i=1}^{N}(1+x_i)(1-x_i+x_i^2).
]
Dividing by
[
M_{1,N}=\prod_{i=1}^{N}(1+x_i)
]
gives
[
P_N
===

\prod_{i=1}^{N}(1-x_i+x_i^2).
]
The formula for $\kappa_N$ follows from
[
\kappa_N=1-P_N.
]
\end{proof}

This identity shows that the large rational expressions produced by
direct symbolic division conceal a simple multiplicative invariant.

% ==================================================================
\section{Cubic Roots of Unity}
% ==================================================================

Let
[
\omega=e^{2\pi i/3}.
]
Then
[
\omega^3=1,
\qquad
\omega\neq1,
\qquad
1+\omega+\omega^2=0.
]

We have
[
(1+\omega x)(1+\omega^2x)
=========================

# 1+(\omega+\omega^2)x+\omega^3x^2

1-x+x^2.
]

Hence:

\begin{theorem}[Root-of-unity representation]
Let
[
E(t)=\prod_{i=1}^{N}(1+x_i t)
=\sum_{k=0}^{N}e_k t^k.
]
Then
[
\boxed{
P_N=E(\omega)E(\omega^2)
}
]
and, for real $x_i$,
[
\boxed{
P_N=\left|E(\omega)\right|^2.
}
]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Using
[
1-x_i+x_i^2
===========

(1+\omega x_i)(1+\omega^2x_i),
]
we obtain
[
P_N
===

\prod_i(1+\omega x_i)
\prod_i(1+\omega^2x_i).
]
The first product is $E(\omega)$ and the second is $E(\omega^2)$.
For real variables,
[
E(\omega^2)=\overline{E(\omega)},
]
which gives the modulus-square representation.
\end{proof}

This is the central structural identity of the paper.

% ==================================================================
\section{Universal Elementary-Symmetric Formula}
% ==================================================================

Since
[
E(\omega)=\sum_{k=0}^{N}e_k\omega^k,
]
and the powers of $\omega$ are periodic modulo $3$, define
[
A_N
===

\sum_{\substack{0\leq k\leq N\k\equiv0\pmod3}}e_k,
]
[
B_N
===

\sum_{\substack{0\leq k\leq N\k\equiv1\pmod3}}e_k,
]
[
C_N
===

\sum_{\substack{0\leq k\leq N\k\equiv2\pmod3}}e_k.
]

Then
[
E(\omega)=A_N+B_N\omega+C_N\omega^2.
]

\begin{theorem}[Universal elementary-symmetric form]
For arbitrary $N$,
[
\boxed{
P_N
===

A_N^2+B_N^2+C_N^2-A_NB_N-A_NC_N-B_NC_N
}
]
and hence
[
\boxed{
\kappa_N
========

1-A_N^2-B_N^2-C_N^2
+A_NB_N+A_NC_N+B_NC_N.
}
]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
We have
[
P_N
===

|A_N+B_N\omega+C_N\omega^2|^2.
]
Since
[
\omega+\omega^2=-1
]
and
[
|\omega|^2=1,
]
direct multiplication gives
[
P_N
===

A_N^2+B_N^2+C_N^2
-A_NB_N-A_NC_N-B_NC_N.
]
Subtracting from $1$ gives the expression for $\kappa_N$.
\end{proof}

An equivalent and sometimes more revealing expression is
[
\boxed{
P_N
===

\frac12
\left[
(A_N-B_N)^2+
(B_N-C_N)^2+
(C_N-A_N)^2
\right].
}
]

Thus
[
\boxed{
\kappa_N
========

1-\frac12
\left[
(A_N-B_N)^2+
(B_N-C_N)^2+
(C_N-A_N)^2
\right].
}
]

This representation makes the modulo-$3$ structure explicit.

% ==================================================================
\section{Recovery of the $N=3,4,5$ Formulae}
% ==================================================================

The symbolic calculations for three, four, and five variables are
special cases of the universal theorem.

For $N=3$,
[
A_3=e_0+e_3=1+e_3,
\qquad
B_3=e_1,
\qquad
C_3=e_2.
]
Substitution gives
[
\boxed{
\kappa_3
========

-e_1^2+e_1e_2+e_1e_3+e_1
-e_2^2+e_2e_3+e_2
-e_3^2-2e_3.
}
]

For $N=4$,
[
A_4=1+e_3,
\qquad
B_4=e_1+e_4,
\qquad
C_4=e_2.
]
Therefore
[
\boxed{
\begin{aligned}
\kappa_4={}&
-e_1^2+e_1e_2+e_1e_3-2e_1e_4+e_1\
&-e_2^2+e_2e_3+e_2e_4+e_2\
&-e_3^2+e_3e_4-2e_3-e_4^2+e_4.
\end{aligned}
}
]

For $N=5$,
[
A_5=1+e_3,
\qquad
B_5=e_1+e_4,
\qquad
C_5=e_2+e_5.
]
Hence
[
\boxed{
\begin{aligned}
\kappa_5={}&
-e_1^2+e_1e_2+e_1e_3-2e_1e_4+e_1e_5+e_1\
&-e_2^2+e_2e_3+e_2e_4-2e_2e_5+e_2\
&-e_3^2+e_3e_4+e_3e_5-2e_3\
&-e_4^2+e_4e_5+e_4-e_5^2+e_5.
\end{aligned}
}
]

These are precisely the exact expressions obtained by direct symbolic
division and elementary-symmetric reduction.

% ==================================================================
\section{Controlled-Variable Transition}
% ==================================================================

The most useful consequence for increasing the number of variables is
the multiplicative transition law.

\begin{theorem}[Exact one-variable transition]
Let
[
\kappa_N
========

1-\prod_{i=1}^{N}(1-x_i+x_i^2).
]
If a new variable $z$ is appended, then
[
\boxed{
1-\kappa_{N+1}
==============

(1-z+z^2)(1-\kappa_N).
}
]
Equivalently,
[
\boxed{
\kappa_{N+1}-\kappa_N
=====================

z(1-z)(1-\kappa_N).
}
]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
From the product representation,
[
1-\kappa_{N+1}
==============

# P_{N+1}

P_N(1-z+z^2).
]
Since
[
P_N=1-\kappa_N,
]
we obtain
[
1-\kappa_{N+1}
==============

(1-z+z^2)(1-\kappa_N).
]
Rearranging,
[
\kappa_{N+1}-\kappa_N
=====================

# \left[1-(1-z+z^2)\right](1-\kappa_N)

z(1-z)(1-\kappa_N).
]
\end{proof}

This explains the experimentally observed quadratic dependence on the
new variable.

Indeed,
[
z(1-z)=z-z^2.
]

% ==================================================================
\section{The Special Values $z=0$ and $z=1$}
% ==================================================================

\begin{corollary}
For every $N$,
[
\boxed{
\kappa_{N+1}(x_1,\ldots,x_N,0)
==============================

\kappa_N(x_1,\ldots,x_N)
}
]
and
[
\boxed{
\kappa_{N+1}(x_1,\ldots,x_N,1)
==============================

\kappa_N(x_1,\ldots,x_N).
}
]
\end{corollary}

\begin{proof}
For $z=0$ or $z=1$,
[
1-z+z^2=1.
]
The result follows immediately from the transition theorem.
\end{proof}

Thus the zeros of
[
z(1-z)
]
are exact fixed points of the extension operation.

% ==================================================================
\section{Multiple Controlled Variables}
% ==================================================================

The one-variable transition iterates immediately.

\begin{theorem}[Exact controlled extension]
Let $r_1,\ldots,r_m$ be appended to an $N$-variable system. Then
[
\boxed{
1-\kappa_{N+m}
==============

(1-\kappa_N)
\prod_{j=1}^{m}(1-r_j+r_j^2).
}
]
Equivalently,
[
\boxed{
\kappa_{N+m}
============

1-(1-\kappa_N)
\prod_{j=1}^{m}(1-r_j+r_j^2).
}
]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Apply the one-variable transition repeatedly:
[
1-\kappa_{N+1}
==============

(1-r_1+r_1^2)(1-\kappa_N),
]
then
[
1-\kappa_{N+2}
==============

(1-r_2+r_2^2)(1-\kappa_{N+1}),
]
and so on.
Multiplication gives the result.
\end{proof}

In particular, for a two-body base system with variables $p,q$,
[
\boxed{
1-\kappa_{p,q,r}
================

(1-\kappa_{p,q})(1-r+r^2)
}
]
and
[
\boxed{
1-\kappa_{p,q,r,s}
==================

(1-\kappa_{p,q})
(1-r+r^2)(1-s+s^2).
}
]

The controlled variables therefore contribute known multiplicative
factors which can, in principle, be divided out.

% ==================================================================
\section{The Two-Factor Case and a Possible Factoring Connection}
% ==================================================================

Suppose
[
N=pq,
]
where $p$ and $q$ are unknown factors of a known integer $N$.

Set
[
u=p+q.
]
The elementary symmetric polynomials are
[
e_1=u,
\qquad
e_2=N.
]

For two variables,
[
P_2=(1-p+p^2)(1-q+q^2).
]
Direct expansion gives
[
\boxed{
P_2
===

u^2-Nu-u+N^2-N+1.
}
]

Hence
[
\boxed{
\kappa_2
========

-u^2+Nu+u-N^2+N.
}
]

Therefore, if $P_2$ or $\kappa_2$ were available independently,
then $u=p+q$ could be obtained from a quadratic equation.

From
[
P_2=u^2-(N+1)u+N^2-N+1,
]
we have
[
\boxed{
u^2-(N+1)u+(N^2-N+1-P_2)=0.
}
]

Once $u$ is known, $p$ and $q$ are the roots of
[
x^2-ux+N=0.
]
Thus
[
\boxed{
p,q
===

\frac{u\pm\sqrt{u^2-4N}}{2}.
}
]

This establishes a precise algebraic connection between the invariant
and factor recovery.

% ==================================================================
\section{Controlled Auxiliary Factors and Factor Recovery}
% ==================================================================

Now suppose
[
N=pq
]
is known but $p,q$ are unknown, while an auxiliary variable $r$ is
under experimental or computational control.

For the three-variable system,
[
1-\kappa_3
==========

(1-\kappa_2)(1-r+r^2).
]

Therefore
[
\boxed{
1-\kappa_2
==========

\frac{1-\kappa_3(r)}
{1-r+r^2}.
}
]

Similarly, for two controlled variables $r,s$,
[
\boxed{
1-\kappa_2
==========

\frac{1-\kappa_4(r,s)}
{(1-r+r^2)(1-s+s^2)}.
}
]

Consequently, if an independent mechanism can provide
$\kappa_3(r)$ or $\kappa_4(r,s)$ without first determining $p$ and $q$,
then the controlled-variable identity gives an exact method for
recovering $\kappa_2$, hence $p+q$, and finally $p,q$.

This leads to the following research problem.

\begin{conjecture}[Invariant-access factoring principle]
If there exists an efficient procedure which, given a composite
integer $N=pq$ and a freely chosen auxiliary value $r$, computes the
corresponding invariant $\kappa_3(p,q,r)$ without implicitly performing
the factorization of $N$, then the transition identity can be used to
recover $p+q$ and hence factor $N$ efficiently.
\end{conjecture}

The conjecture is intentionally stated as an algorithmic conditional.
The algebraic identities proved in this paper do \emph{not} by
themselves establish an efficient factoring algorithm.

% ==================================================================
\section{Why the Controlled Variables Are Interesting}
% ==================================================================

The controlled-variable construction provides a natural normalization
test.

For every admissible $r$,
[
\frac{1-\kappa_3(r)}
{1-r+r^2}
=========

1-\kappa_2.
]

Thus the right-hand side is independent of $r$.

For two controlled variables,
[
\frac{1-\kappa_4(r,s)}
{(1-r+r^2)(1-s+s^2)}
====================

1-\kappa_2.
]

Hence measurements or computations at different control values should
collapse onto the same invariant.

For example,
[
\frac{1-\kappa_3(r_1)}
{1-r_1+r_1^2}
=============

\frac{1-\kappa_3(r_2)}
{1-r_2+r_2^2}.
]

Likewise,
[
\frac{1-\kappa_4(r_1,s_1)}
{(1-r_1+r_1^2)(1-s_1+s_1^2)}
============================

\frac{1-\kappa_4(r_2,s_2)}
{(1-r_2+r_2^2)(1-s_2+s_2^2)}.
]

This makes controlled auxiliary variables useful as probes of the same
underlying two-body quantity.

% ==================================================================
\section{Interpretation of the Elementary-Symmetric Pattern}
% ==================================================================

The original symbolic results show apparently complicated
coefficients such as
[
e_1e_2,\quad e_1e_3,\quad -2e_1e_4,\quad e_1e_5,
]
and analogous terms involving other $e_i$.

The root-of-unity representation explains these coefficients.

Since
[
P_N
===

\sum_{i,j}e_i e_j\omega^{i-j},
]
the coefficient associated with a pair $(i,j)$ depends only on
[
i-j\pmod3.
]

Therefore the coefficient pattern is not accidental and does not
require separate derivation for every $N$.

The three residue classes
[
k\equiv0,1,2\pmod3
]
are the fundamental structural objects.

This observation replaces the apparent complexity of the expanded
polynomials by a three-class decomposition.

% ==================================================================
\section{Generalization to Higher Powers}
% ==================================================================

The cubic case is part of a broader root-of-unity construction.

For a positive integer $m$,
[
1+x^m
]
can be related to roots of unity and corresponding cyclotomic factors.
More generally, expressions of the form
[
\frac{\prod_i(1+x_i^m)}
{\prod_i(1+x_i)}
]
can be analyzed using roots of unity and evaluations of
[
E(t)=\prod_i(1+x_i t).
]

The present paper focuses on $m=3$, because
[
1-x+x^2
]
is a quadratic factor and the corresponding root-of-unity structure
produces an especially simple quadratic invariant.

A natural direction for future work is to determine which other
cyclotomic quotients produce similarly low-dimensional symmetric
invariants.

% ==================================================================
\section{Computational Verification}
% ==================================================================

The symbolic calculations for $N=3,4,5$ provide exact verification of
the general theorem.

The following properties were verified algebraically:

\begin{enumerate}
\item Exact cancellation of $X_N/M_{1,N}$ for $N=3,4,5$.
\item Exact construction of $\kappa_N=1-P_N$.
\item Exact elementary-symmetric reduction.
\item Zero symmetrization remainder.
\item Exact equality between direct and symmetric representations.
\item Exact $3\to4$ transition.
\item Exact $4\to5$ transition.
\item Quadratic dependence on the new variable.
\item Vanishing of the transition at $n=0$ and $n=1$.
\end{enumerate}

The symbolic results are therefore consistent with the all-$N$
factorization theorem proved above.

Importantly, however, the all-$N$ theorem itself does not require
computer algebra. It follows directly from
[
1+x^3=(1+x)(1-x+x^2)
]
and the cubic-root-of-unity factorization.

% ==================================================================
\section{Limitations Regarding Integer Factorization}
% ==================================================================

It is important to distinguish an exact invariant from an efficient
factorization algorithm.

For a known composite integer
[
N=pq,
]
the value
[
\kappa_2
]
contains information about $p+q$. If $\kappa_2$ can be obtained without
knowing $p$ and $q$, factor recovery follows through a quadratic
equation.

However, if calculating $\kappa_2$ from its defining expression
requires knowledge of $p$ and $q$, then no computational advantage has
been obtained.

Likewise, adding controlled variables $r,s$ does not by itself solve
the problem. The identities
[
1-\kappa_3
==========

(1-\kappa_2)(1-r+r^2)
]
and
[
1-\kappa_4
==========

(1-\kappa_2)(1-r+r^2)(1-s+s^2)
]
only become algorithmically significant if the corresponding
higher-body invariant is independently accessible.

Thus the open problem is not the algebraic identity. The identity is
proved. The open problem is whether there exists a useful,
non-circular way to access the invariant for a composite integer.

% ==================================================================
\section{Research Program}
% ==================================================================

The results suggest the following sequence of investigations.

\subsection{All-$N$ symbolic verification}

Implement the formula
[
\kappa_N
========

1-A_N^2-B_N^2-C_N^2+A_NB_N+A_NC_N+B_NC_N
]
and verify it automatically for larger $N$ without expanding the
original quotient.

\subsection{Factor-recovery experiment}

For selected composite integers
[
N=pq,
]
calculate the invariant using several controlled values $r$ and verify
the exact normalization
[
\frac{1-\kappa_3(r)}{1-r+r^2}
=============================

1-\kappa_2.
]

\subsection{Non-circular accessibility}

The central computational question should then be:

\begin{quote}
Can the quantity $\kappa_3(N,r)$ be computed from $N$ and $r$
without first determining $p$ and $q$?
\end{quote}

If the answer is no for the direct polynomial definition, investigate
whether an alternative representation, oracle, recurrence, modular
evaluation, or physical interpretation supplies the required
information.

\subsection{Controlled two-variable probes}

Study
[
\kappa_4(N,r,s)
]
and determine whether varying $(r,s)$ produces information that is not
already available from the direct evaluation of the composite integer.

\subsection{Complexity analysis}

If an accessible form is found, compare its computational complexity
with classical factoring algorithms. In particular, determine whether
the invariant merely repackages the same difficulty as factoring or
actually provides a new efficient route.

% ==================================================================
\section{Conclusion}
% ==================================================================

The calculations for three, four, and five variables reveal an exact
all-dimensional structure.

The defining quotient
[
P_N
===

\frac{\prod_i(1+x_i^3)}
{\prod_i(1+x_i)}
]
reduces identically to
[
\boxed{
P_N=\prod_i(1-x_i+x_i^2).
}
]

Using a nontrivial cubic root of unity $\omega$,
[
1-x+x^2=(1+\omega x)(1+\omega^2x),
]
so that
[
\boxed{
P_N=E(\omega)E(\omega^2)
}
]
where
[
E(t)=\sum_{k=0}^{N}e_k t^k.
]

Grouping the elementary symmetric polynomials by index modulo $3$
gives the universal formula
[
\boxed{
\kappa_N
========

1-A_N^2-B_N^2-C_N^2
+A_NB_N+A_NC_N+B_NC_N.
}
]

The transition under adjoining a controlled variable $z$ is exactly
[
\boxed{
\kappa_{N+1}-\kappa_N
=====================

z(1-z)(1-\kappa_N).
}
]

For several controlled variables,
[
\boxed{
1-\kappa_{N+m}
==============

(1-\kappa_N)
\prod_{j=1}^{m}(1-r_j+r_j^2).
}
]

Thus the apparent complexity of the $3\to4\to5$ transitions is
resolved by a simple multiplicative law.

For the two-factor problem $N=pq$, the invariant is directly related
to $p+q$, so access to the invariant could in principle lead to
factor recovery. The controlled-variable identities show precisely
how auxiliary variables can be divided out.

The remaining question is algorithmic rather than algebraic:

[
\boxed{
\textit{Can the relevant invariant be accessed efficiently without
already knowing the hidden factors?}
}
]

Answering that question is the natural next stage of this research.

\bigskip

\noindent
\textbf{Status of the results.}
The algebraic identities and all-$N$ formulae presented here are exact
theorems. The proposed connection to integer factorization is a
research direction and does not constitute, by itself, a proven
polynomial-time factoring algorithm.

